{"id":"atproto-hg3","title":"Replace /_magic/check-email with protected /_internal/account-by-email","description":"## Problem

The `/_magic/check-email` endpoint on pds-core returns `{ exists: true/false, did: "..." }` for any email with no authentication. It is an email enumeration oracle. It also reads from the `account_email` mirror table in `magic-pds.sqlite`, which we are eliminating as part of the broader migration to use `account.sqlite` as the single source of truth.

**Identified in the [magic-pds security review](https://gist.github.com/s-adamantine/c88df4fc940259cfe903e9de474f07fc#critical-vulnerabilities) as MED-4.**

See also: `better-auth-migration-plan.md` Phase 0.4

## Solution

Replace `/_magic/check-email` with `/_internal/account-by-email`, which:
- Requires `x-internal-secret` header matching `MAGIC_INTERNAL_SECRET` env var
- Queries `pds.ctx.accountManager.getAccountByEmail()` directly — the upstream PDS already has this as a public method on AccountManager (in `node_modules/@atproto/pds/src/account-manager/account-manager.ts`). It queries `account.sqlite` directly — no mirror table needed.
- Returns `{ did: string } | { did: null }`

## Implementation

1. In `packages/pds-core/src/index.ts`, add the new endpoint:

```typescript
app.get("/_internal/account-by-email", async (req, res) => {
  if (req.headers["x-internal-secret"] !== process.env.MAGIC_INTERNAL_SECRET) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: "Unauthorized" });
  }
  const email = req.query.email as string;
  if (!email) return res.status(400).json({ error: "Missing email" });
  const account = await pds.ctx.accountManager.getAccountByEmail(email);
  res.json({ did: account?.did ?? null });
});
```

2. Update auth-service to call `/_internal/account-by-email` with `x-internal-secret` header instead of calling `/_magic/check-email`.

3. Delete `/_magic/check-email` from pds-core.

4. Add `MAGIC_INTERNAL_SECRET` to `.env.example`.

## Files to modify

- `packages/pds-core/src/index.ts` — add new endpoint, delete old one
- `packages/auth-service/src/routes/` (any file calling check-email) — update to new endpoint
- `.env.example` — add MAGIC_INTERNAL_SECRET

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] `/_internal/account-by-email` requires x-internal-secret header
- [ ] Unauthenticated calls return 401
- [ ] Valid calls return `{ did: "..." }` or `{ did: null }`
- [ ] `/_magic/check-email` is deleted
- [ ] Auth-service uses the new endpoint successfully","status":"open","priority":1,"issue_type":"feature","owner":"atproto@adamspiers.org","created_at":"2026-02-20T02:15:22Z","created_by":"Adam Spiers","updated_at":"2026-02-20T02:15:22Z"}

{"id":"atproto-ke8","title":"Prevent flash of email login form when login_hint is present","description":"When /oauth/authorize is called with a login_hint email, the current implementation renders the full login page (including the email input step) and then client-side JS transitions to the OTP step after sending the OTP. This causes a brief flash of the email input form before the OTP step appears, which is confusing to users who already entered their email in the client app.

Fix: when login_hint is a valid email, send the OTP server-side during the GET /oauth/authorize handler and render the page already in the OTP step (hiding the email step entirely via HTML/CSS). No client-side transition needed — the OTP step should be immediately visible with no flash.","notes":"When this issue is resolved, also remove the 'known issue: brief flash of email form' caveat from README.md (Flow 1 description) and from docs/flows.md.

A partial fix exists in git stash: 'atproto-ke8: partial flash fix (server-side step visibility, OTP still client-side)'. What it achieves: renders step-email hidden and step-otp visible server-side when login_hint is present, pre-fills the masked email subtitle, pre-fills the hidden email input, and shows the recovery link. The flash is eliminated visually. What it does NOT do: OTP is still sent client-side via JS on page load (sendOtp called in DOMContentLoaded). The full fix requires moving OTP dispatch into the server-side GET handler before rendering the page. See atproto-trf for investigation into that approach.","status":"open","priority":2,"issue_type":"bug","owner":"atproto@adamspiers.org","created_at":"2026-02-21T01:11:25Z","created_by":"Adam Spiers","updated_at":"2026-02-21T11:36:29Z"}

